---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { loadAll, displayImages } from "../lib/data.js";
const { terms } = loadAll();
const count = terms.reduce((n, t) => n + displayImages(t).length, 0);
---

<Base title="About the archive">
  <header class="page-heading">
    <p class="meta">About the reference</p>
    <h1>
      A way of seeing.
      <br />A place to begin<span class="red-period">.</span>
    </h1>
    <p>
      Style Index brings visual characteristics, historical context and
      production methods into one browsable reference.
    </p>
  </header>
  <div class="about-grid">
    <div>
      <section>
        <h2>Read beyond the label.</h2>
        <p>
          A movement is a historical and social context. A style is a
          recognizable visual language. An aesthetic is a looser grouping of
          visual preferences. A technique describes how something is made; an
          apparatus is the equipment involved. A letterform tradition concerns
          the construction of writing and type.
        </p>
        <p>
          Keeping these distinctions makes it possible to compare related ideas
          without pretending they are the same kind of thing.
        </p>
      </section>
      <section>
        <h2>Use it as a working reference.</h2>
        <ol>
          <li>
            Search a name or a visual quality such as “geometric,” “layered” or
            “contour.”
          </li>
          <li>
            Refine by type, grid, palette, contrast, texture and ornament.
          </li>
          <li>
            Open an entry for its signature, context, practical guidance and
            sources.
          </li>
          <li>
            Compare two entries to understand their differences. Save useful
            entries in this browser.
          </li>
        </ol>
      </section>
      <section>
        <h2>What is in the archive?</h2>
        <p>
          {terms.length} entries currently have practical visual guides.{" "}
          {terms.filter((t) => t.summary).length} also have historical
          overviews. {count} images have been selected for display. Coverage is
          strongest in European and North American graphic design, with selected
          Japanese traditions and digital aesthetics.
        </p>
        <p>
          This is a growing reference, not an exhaustive global history. The
          gaps matter: additional regional histories, non-Latin typography and
          more primary-source examples are needed.
        </p>
      </section>
    </div>
    <div>
      <section>
        <h2>Evidence and interpretation.</h2>
        <p>
          Historical claims have recorded sources. The supplied research,{" "}
          <em>Graphic Design Movements 1850–2000</em>, informed the expansion.
          Its useful structure has been retained, while overly broad synonyms,
          dubious examples and unsupported claims have been corrected or
          omitted.
        </p>
        <p>
          Entries distinguish material adapted from this research from primary
          collection records. A source reference does not mean that every claim
          in the supplied report has been independently verified.
        </p>
        <p>
          Visual signatures, suggested uses and comparisons are editorial
          observations. They are tools for looking and making, not historical
          claims or rigid rules.
        </p>
      </section>
      <section>
        <h2>Images have a context.</h2>
        <p>
          Only approved image selections appear in the interface. Unreviewed
          search results remain outside the gallery, and rejected examples are
          excluded. Every displayed image retains its source, attribution and
          rights information.
        </p>
        <p>
          Index thumbnails are monochrome to keep browsing consistent. Entry
          pages show original color by default and offer a monochrome study
          view. Always consult the original before making color judgments.
        </p>
      </section>
      <section>
        <h2>Dates are not borders.</h2>
        <p>
          Approximate active periods are marked with “c.” They describe a useful
          historical frame, not the first and last time a visual language
          existed. Entries without supported dates remain outside the timeline
          rather than receiving a guessed date.
        </p>
      </section>
      <section>
        <h2>Private by design.</h2>
        <p>
          Search and comparison run in your browser. Saved entries are stored
          locally on this device; there is no account or cross-device
          synchronization.
        </p>
      </section>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>
